// opencode.jsonc — global opencode configuration, managed by home-manager.
//
// Edit HERE (devrc/scripts/opencode/opencode.jsonc) then `home-manager switch`
// (or scripts/ship.sh). ~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc is a READ-ONLY store
// symlink — editing it directly is impossible, and editing it "successfully"
// would mean the symlink is gone and the file has drifted.
//
// ==========================================================================
// 🔴 TWO LAYERS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS A SAFETY CONTROL.
// ==========================================================================
//
//   LAYER 1 — this file's `permission.bash` block: a leading wildcard `allow`
//     plus a short DENY backstop, and NOTHING else. It used to also carry a
//     third verdict — broad `ask` on mutation families: FRICTION, so a human
//     saw the command — but `ask` is structurally a KILL SWITCH under
//     `opencode run` (auto-reject → mid-run abandonment; it killed real
//     dispatches), and on 2026-09-20 the operator chose auto-approve-everything
//     for unattended dispatch instead. ALL 52 ask rules were deleted that day.
//     There is no prompt layer any more: the only things standing between an
//     agent and an action are the denies below and layer 2.
//
//   LAYER 2 — ~/.config/opencode/plugin/guard.js -> guard_core.py: ENFORCEMENT.
//     It PARSES: splits on `;`/`&&`/`||`/`|`/`&`, strips `VAR=…` prefixes and
//     `sudo`/`doas`/`env`/`timeout`/… wrappers, recurses into `bash -c '…'`,
//     and reasons about argv. It throws from `tool.execute.before`, which hard-
//     blocks the call. Since 2026-09-20 this is the ONLY enforcement layer.
//
// WHY LAYER 2 EXISTS AT ALL (history — the reason guard_core exists, kept
// because the next "add a glob and feel safe" temptation is the same one).
// Globs match a command node's FULL TEXT, so "this command wipes a node" is not
// expressible — the set of spellings is unbounded. Two rounds of
// pattern-patching each closed the spellings we thought of and left the ones we
// did not. MEASURED at c1e4c02, all resolving ALLOW on the primary agent:
//     talosctl -n 192.168.50.94 reset          (a node wipe, no prompt)
//     talosctl --nodes=192.168.50.94 reset
//     rm -f -r /        rm --recursive --force /
//     mke2fs /dev/sdc   mkswap /dev/sdd
// Those families are now held by guard_core (talosctl reset, recursive rm of
// critical targets, dd to a block device, git stash/reset/add -A/clean) — and
// the REST (kubectl mutations, sudo, systemctl, secrets decryption) is covered
// by NOTHING, by explicit operator decision.
//
// 🔴 SO: DO NOT ADD A NEW DANGEROUS FAMILY HERE AND CONSIDER IT HANDLED. A glob
// added here buys NOTHING: an `allow` row is a no-op under the leading
// wildcard, and an `ask` row kills unattended runs mid-task. If the action is
// irreversible, add a check to guard_core.py's "opencode" policy — that is the
// layer with a parser and the layer with a test matrix over nine prefixes.
//
// WHAT THE DENY BLOCK BELOW IS FOR (honestly): it is a short, deliberately
// NON-EXHAUSTIVE mirror of the guard's families in their naive spellings. It
// exists ONLY so that a failure to load guard.js does not silently remove every
// deny at once. It is defence in depth, not the control — every one of these
// patterns has known bypasses, which is the whole reason layer 2 exists.
//
// ==========================================================================
// Mechanics of layer 1 (measured on v1.18.29 — do not re-derive)
// ==========================================================================
//
// 🔴 PERMISSION ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS THE INVERSE OF CLAUDE CODE.
// opencode evaluates permission globs with LAST-MATCH-WINS over a FLAT ORDERED
// ARRAY (`opencode debug agent <name>` prints that array — it is the authority,
// not this file). So `"*": "allow"` must come FIRST and every deny AFTER it.
// Do not interleave or "tidy" these keys. scripts/tests/test_opencode_config.py
// pins the EFFECTIVE RESOLVED OUTCOME for exactly this reason; a key-order
// assertion alone is vacuous.
//
// 🔴 EVERY PATTERN IS LEADING-`*` AND INFIX-`*`. opencode matches a command
// node's FULL TEXT, so a bare `"git stash*"` is bypassed by any prefix
// (`FOO=1 git stash`, `sudo -n git stash`, `git -C /tmp stash`) and a
// `"kubectl delete*"` by any INTERLEAVED FLAG (`kubectl -n foo delete pod`).
// `*` crosses spaces, `/` and `-`, so `*git*stash*` catches every ordering.
// The deny block keeps that style for exactly the reason the asks once did.
//
// 🔴 HISTORY OF THE ASK LAYER (deleted 2026-09-20; the measurements live in git
// history and in test_opencode_config.py's characterization tests):
//   * `ask` SEMANTICS (NOT re-derived at the pin, v1.18.16; observing needs a
//     run that EXECUTES, which the engine tests refuse to do): `opencode run`
//     AUTO-REJECTS an `ask` (prints "auto-rejecting"); only the interactive TUI
//     turns one into a prompt, and `opencode debug agent --tool` AUTO-APPROVES
//     it. So `ask` was "friction for a human", never a control on an
//     unattended agent — and for `run` it was pure mid-run abandonment.
//   * The narrowing sagas: `*age*-d*` and `*rm*-r*` were infix rules whose
//     substring collisions ("age" ⊂ package/message/storage…, "rm" ⊂
//     format/terraform/confirm…) auto-rejected ordinary read-only commands and
//     killed real dispatches (2026-08-21/22). They were narrowed to the literal
//     `*age -d*` / `*rm -r*` forms, each narrowing opening known gaps that were
//     pinned as characterization tests rather than closed. All of that is gone
//     with the layer: the families are either guard-held or ungated by design.
//
// Other measured facts baked into this file (v1.18.29, EXCEPT where a bullet
// says otherwise — the 2026-08-19 re-derivation could not reach three of them,
// and a claim relabelled to a version nothing measured it on is worse than a
// stale one, because it looks fresh):
//   * only a `"*": "deny"` removes a tool from the request schema; a narrow
//     deny does not, and `"ask"`/`"allow"` cost exactly what each other costs.
//   * `small_model` SCOPE (NOT re-derived at the pin, v1.18.16): it drives
//     TITLE GENERATION ONLY — not compaction. The real cheap-model lever is
//     pinning the hidden title/summary/compaction agents in the `agent` block
//     below.
//   * a tool listed in this global `permission` block is re-enabled on the
//     HIDDEN title/summary/compaction agents too, whose stock tool set is
//     EMPTY. Hence the explicit `"*": "deny"` on each of them below.
//   * DEPRECATED, deliberately absent — a config-SCHEMA claim about keys this
//     file does NOT set, which the resolved `debug agent` dumps structurally
//     cannot see, so it was carried forward at v1.18.16 rather than relabelled:
//     agent-level `tools`, `mode` (at config level), `layout`, `autoshare`,
//     `reference` (now `references`), `maxSteps` (now `steps`).
//     `theme`/`keybinds`/`tui` moved to a separate ~/.config/opencode/tui.json
//     and are NOT set here.
//   * there is NO `list` tool and NO `websearch` tool on 1.18.29. The real set is
//     exactly {bash, edit, glob, grep, invalid, question, read, skill, task,
//     todowrite, webfetch, write}. Naming a nonexistent tool here is a no-op.
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",

  // 2026-09-20: default moved from openrouter/xiaomi/mimo-v2.5 (default since
  // 2026-08) to z-ai/glm-5.3-flash. opencode-dispatch takes NO default alias —
  // with no `-m` it omits the flag entirely and lands here.
  "model": "openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash",
  // Title generation only. Compaction is pinned via the `agent` block.
  "small_model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",

  "autoupdate": false,
  "share": "disabled",
  "snapshot": true,
  "lsp": true,
  "formatter": true,
  "subagent_depth": 2,

  "compaction": {
    "auto": true,
    "prune": true,
    "reserved": 16000
  },

  // Cap a single tool result so one `kubectl get -A -o yaml` cannot blow the
  // window and force an immediate compaction.
  "tool_output": {
    "max_lines": 800,
    "max_bytes": 24576
  },

  "watcher": {
    "ignore": [
      "node_modules/**",
      ".git/**",
      "result/**",
      ".direnv/**",
      "**/*.enc.yaml"
    ]
  },

  "agent": {
    // The three HIDDEN system agents. These run on every session (titles) or on
    // every context overflow (summary/compaction) and have no reasoning
    // requirement — pinning them to the flash model is the actual cost lever,
    // since `small_model` above only covers title generation.
    //
    // 🔴 `"permission": {"*": "deny"}` is NOT optional. Their STOCK tool set is
    // EMPTY (measured: `enabled=[]` against a bare config dir), but every entry
    // in the global `permission` block below is appended to THEIR resolved list
    // too — which re-enabled bash, write, edit, task and skill on all three.
    // compaction runs automatically on every context overflow, so that handed a
    // shell and a writer to the cheap model on a path nobody watches, and made
    // every title generation carry the ~3,730-token skill catalogue.
    "title": {
      "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
      "permission": { "*": "deny" }
    },
    "summary": {
      "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
      "permission": { "*": "deny" }
    },
    "compaction": {
      "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
      "permission": { "*": "deny" }
    },

    // Built-in `plan` is only nominally read-only — it can still write and
    // shell out. Make it GENUINELY read-only so "let's plan this" cannot
    // mutate the tree.
    //
    // `task` and `write` are here because the global block below OVERRODE the
    // built-ins: stock `plan` resolves `task 'general' deny` (it may not spawn
    // the general subagent, which HAS a shell), and the global `"task": "allow"`
    // landed after it and flipped it to allow. Restating them at agent level
    // puts them last again.
    "plan": {
      "permission": {
        "edit": "deny",
        "write": "deny",
        "bash": "deny",
        "task": { "general": "deny" }
      }
    }
  },

  "permission": {
    // NOTE: `read` is deliberately ABSENT. opencode ships a built-in
    // `read: {"*": allow, "*.env": ask, "*.env.*": ask, "*.env.example": allow}`
    // — a blanket `"read": "allow"` here lands AFTER those and silently defeats
    // the `.env` guard on every agent. The default already allows everything
    // except `.env`, so there is nothing to add.
    // `list` and `websearch` are absent because NO SUCH TOOLS EXIST on 1.18.29.
    "glob": "allow",
    "grep": "allow",
    "edit": "allow",
    "write": "allow",
    "webfetch": "allow",
    "todowrite": "allow",
    "task": "allow",
    "skill": "allow",

    // 🔴 2026-09-20, operator decision: the prompt layer is OFF — every
    // permission request is auto-approved. `doom_loop` and `external_directory`
    // were the last non-bash `ask`s; both flipped to `allow` in the same
    // decision that deleted the 52 bash `ask` rules (see the bash block).
    // Consequences, stated plainly: an agent can loop on one call without a
    // prompt, and can read/write ANY path outside the project directory.
    "doom_loop": "allow",

    // Was {"*": "ask", "/home/zach/.config/opencode/skills/**": "allow"} — the
    // 2026-08-23 carve-out that stopped a skill's own reference/ read from
    // auto-rejecting and killing a dispatch mid-task (#761; preflight gained a
    // config-state line for it in #789). The map is gone with the decision:
    // EVERY path outside --dir is now reachable without a prompt. Reversing
    // this means restoring the map AND re-checking
    // `opencode-dispatch preflight`'s skill-reference verdict line.
    "external_directory": "allow",

    "bash": {
      // 🔴 2026-09-20: the 52 `ask` rules that used to sit between this
      // wildcard and the deny block were DELETED — auto-approve-everything was
      // chosen so unattended dispatches stop dying on auto-rejected prompts.
      // Re-adding one is a mid-run kill switch under `opencode run`. The deny
      // block below is the only layer-1 control left, and it is defence in
      // depth, not the control (see the header).
      "*": "allow",

      // ================= DENY: naive-spelling backstop ONLY ================ //
      // 🔴 READ THE HEADER. These are NOT airtight and are NOT the control.
      // Every one has known bypasses (that is the measured history of this
      // file). They exist so that a guard.js that fails to load does not
      // silently remove every deny at once. The enforcement lives in
      // guard_core.py's "opencode" policy, which parses argv.
      "*git*add -A*": "deny",
      "*git*add --all*": "deny",
      "*git*add .": "deny",
      "*git*stash*": "deny",
      "*git*reset --hard*": "deny",
      "*git*clean -f*": "deny",
      "*rm -rf /*": "deny",
      "*rm -rf ~*": "deny",
      "*rm -rf $HOME*": "deny",
      "*talosctl*reset*": "deny",
      "*mkfs*": "deny",
      "*mke2fs*": "deny",
      "*mkswap*": "deny"
    }
  }
}
